WEBVTT

00:00:02.630 --> 00:00:06.120
<i>Sixteen months after Kusakabe-sensei
first took charge of our concert band,</i>

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:09.400
<i>we made it through the district
and prefectural qualifiers</i>

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:13.320
<i>and progressed to the Tokai competition,
the final stage of the Category B All-Japans.</i>

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:22.750
Don't get sentimental yet, Chika-chan.

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:25.700
We still have Tokai.

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:26.080
Yeah.

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:29.500
Thank you, Serizawa-san.

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:30.990
For what?

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:34.380
For cheering us on through both
the district and prefectural qualifiers.

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:38.100
I was just worried my
good-for-nothing student would fail.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:40.990
Why don't you just take the stage with us?

00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:41.810
Give it a rest!

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:44.200
Tell me how you really feel, Nao-chan!

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:47.680
You can kiss my private lessons goodbye!

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:49.360
Wait, I take that back!

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:52.030
I'm sorry, Serizawa-sensei!

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:32.360
What, you want to join?

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:36.170
I'm only here because Homura-san asked me
to listen to the whole band rehearse.

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:37.670
Idle hands are just what I need right now.

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:38.550
Come here a sec.

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:45.510
Kusakabe-sensei said he'd be a bit late...

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:55.910
Some of the first-years are having trouble
syncing up, so she's playing the piece for them.

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:01.850
This piano's out of tune.

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:03.410
Is it?

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:06.690
See?

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:09.190
Sure you're not just nitpicking?

00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:12.310
Listening to an accurate rendition
is the fastest way to get better.

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:14.900
Whatever. We'll get Sensei to fix it.

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:17.500
You're still relying on him for everything?

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:19.090
We're not relying on him.

00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:21.240
We're sucking him dry.

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:22.320
You piece of trash.

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:23.340
Say what?

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:25.820
Calm down, both of you.

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:30.670
I didn't know high schoolers
these days were so finicky.

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:38.030
A sharp pianist would be able to wring some
great notes out of that keyboard, you know.

00:03:38.030 --> 00:03:39.650
And who might you be?

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:41.320
Then you do it.

00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:44.150
Cheeky brat.

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:47.390
Shall we have a duel, then?

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:50.880
If I win, buy me something to eat.

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:52.620
I haven't had a thing all day.

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:55.470
I don't know who you are,
but we have to practise—

00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:57.540
And what if I win?

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:00.680
I'll tune that piano for free.

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:01.640
You're on.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:03.670
If I lose, my friend Homura-san
will buy you a jam bun or something.

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:05.290
She called me her friend...

00:04:05.290 --> 00:04:06.120
If I lose, my friend Homura-san
will buy you a jam bun or something.

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:08.010
Wait, I'm paying?!

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:25.070
Wow.

00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:29.700
She's playing while avoiding
the keys that are out of tune.

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:37.560
Oh, she plays the melodica?

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:39.880
She's taking her on with that?

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:43.120
So she never intended to
face her fair and square?

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:45.930
She's just as childish as Serizawa-san.

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:03.690
She's so good...

00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:09.320
Ah...

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:23.220
That sounded totally different
from the pianicas we're used to.

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:27.670
This is a clavietta made in Italy in the 1960s.

00:05:27.670 --> 00:05:31.140
It's a bit peculiar for a reed instrument.

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:33.600
She hates loneliness.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:37.270
So she creates music that you
can't forget once you listen to it.

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:40.270
I'd like to know your name, if you don't mind.

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:43.240
It's Yamanobe.

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:45.480
Mail-order melodicas?

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:47.400
Could you be...

00:05:48.190 --> 00:05:52.520
You're Snufskin, a lover of true
freedom and music, aren't you?

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:56.700
Take me to Moominvalley with you!

00:05:56.700 --> 00:05:57.950
There, there.

00:05:57.950 --> 00:05:59.750
We've got practice now.

00:06:01.310 --> 00:06:05.750
You could declare you won, but you won't, huh?

00:06:06.170 --> 00:06:09.870
I'll take back what I said earlier
about your being a cheeky brat.

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:10.890
Makoto!

00:06:11.830 --> 00:06:15.180
I've been waiting in the staff room ever since
you said out of the blue that you were coming.

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:16.530
Please.

00:06:16.530 --> 00:06:19.850
You're the one who wanted to see me, Shinjirou.

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:24.220
I wasn't expecting you to show up
on my doorstep at school today.

00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:26.280
So, what do you want?

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:27.820
I can't talk about it here.

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:32.110
I'll wait until you're done with rehearsal, then.

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:34.530
But first...

00:06:36.380 --> 00:06:38.340
Homura-san, one bun, stat!

00:06:40.750 --> 00:06:43.500
Please wait just five minutes
before starting rehearsal!

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:45.370
W-Wait, Chika-chan!

00:06:45.370 --> 00:06:46.800
I'll go with you!

00:06:50.210 --> 00:06:52.090
What's wrong, Haruta?

00:06:52.760 --> 00:06:54.310
My heart has been torn to shreds.

00:06:55.580 --> 00:06:59.020
They're on a first-name basis.
Just what is their relationship?

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:01.890
That's what I'd like to know too.

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:04.730
Go by yourself, Chika-chan.

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:07.770
I'll calm myself down here.

00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:10.590
What did you even come with me for?

00:07:12.910 --> 00:07:15.440
Your conducting style
sure has changed, Shinjirou!

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:16.880
Come on!

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:17.910
Okay, okay.

00:07:18.580 --> 00:07:19.820
Yamanobe Makoto.

00:07:20.870 --> 00:07:25.170
If my memory serves, Kusakabe-sensei's
teacher was named Yamanobe Fujihiko.

00:07:25.870 --> 00:07:27.040
Yamanobe...

00:07:27.510 --> 00:07:32.640
He's deceased now, but he used to be
a flutist and a famous conductor.

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:36.490
He had a granddaughter who
was called a piano virtuoso.

00:07:36.490 --> 00:07:37.930
Could that be her?

00:07:37.930 --> 00:07:41.760
I don't know or care if she's the
granddaughter of Sensei's teacher.

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:43.800
I'll expose that vixen's true colours!

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:45.710
W-Wait, Haruta!

00:07:57.720 --> 00:08:03.660
The reception area and principal's office
were originally one room, so the wall is thin.

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:07.070
And today's Saturday,
which means the principal isn't here.

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:09.400
It's locked?

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:12.460
But I don't have time for this! Sensei's in danger!

00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:14.280
Should we get the keys from the staff room?

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:17.400
Do you really think they'd give us
the key to the principal's office?

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:18.730
Hello? Kamijou here.

00:08:20.090 --> 00:08:22.090
I have a favour to ask.

00:08:24.890 --> 00:08:28.500
There is no lock in this world we cannot open.

00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:29.310
But of course.

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:32.110
Who are those criminals?

00:08:32.110 --> 00:08:34.320
The Hagimoto brothers, of the inventor club.

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:37.470
Thank you!

00:08:37.470 --> 00:08:41.810
Graham Bell must be smiling
down on you from heaven!

00:08:41.810 --> 00:08:43.870
As if!

00:08:43.870 --> 00:08:46.870
It's only a matter of time before
the Yamanobe family goes bankrupt.

00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:48.990
You reap what you sow.

00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:52.250
It's all my father's fault for
recklessly dabbling in real estate.

00:08:52.680 --> 00:09:00.350
Yamanobe-sensei's beloved Bösendorfer
imperial model was found at his German villa.

00:09:00.820 --> 00:09:04.470
Your father is trying to auction it off.

00:09:05.110 --> 00:09:07.000
A Bösendorfer imperial model?

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:08.150
To think that the piano
was at the villa in Germany...

00:09:08.150 --> 00:09:09.430
Impala?
To think that the piano
was at the villa in Germany...

00:09:09.430 --> 00:09:10.190
I guess at least it wasn't here in Japan,
what with the humidity and all.

00:09:10.190 --> 00:09:12.400
Imperial. Means "majestic".

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:12.690
It's at your parents' house now.

00:09:12.690 --> 00:09:14.270
The emperor of pianos.
It's at your parents' house now.

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:19.410
It's a 1912-make 97-key imperial model.

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:23.310
Designed by architect Josef Hoffman,

00:09:23.310 --> 00:09:26.460
it has an ivory keyboard,
which is extremely rare nowadays.

00:09:26.910 --> 00:09:31.420
If auctioned off, it could probably
fetch upwards of twenty million yen.

00:09:31.420 --> 00:09:32.780
Twenty million?!

00:09:32.780 --> 00:09:34.170
They can do what they want.

00:09:34.170 --> 00:09:36.420
I've been disowned by my parents,

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:38.270
so I couldn't care less.

00:09:39.830 --> 00:09:41.800
There's just one problem.

00:09:41.800 --> 00:09:46.140
That's why your father contacted you through me.

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:46.890
What?

00:09:47.370 --> 00:09:51.520
They found the Bösendorfer, but not its key.

00:09:52.180 --> 00:09:57.240
If they had to replace the lock,
it would lose millions in value.

00:09:57.240 --> 00:09:59.080
So that's what this is about.

00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:00.110
How ridiculous.

00:10:00.110 --> 00:10:06.340
Yamanobe-sensei wrote in his will that he told
the key's whereabouts to you and you alone.

00:10:06.340 --> 00:10:09.830
What? I have no idea where it is!

00:10:09.830 --> 00:10:13.170
He left you the clavietta.

00:10:13.170 --> 00:10:17.170
I've disassembled it plenty of times.
There was no key inside.

00:10:17.170 --> 00:10:20.130
Well, that's a problem.

00:10:21.670 --> 00:10:25.140
Giving me trouble until the very end...

00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:28.050
It's just like Grandpa to write such a selfish will.

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:32.920
Yamanobe-sensei made too many
enemies due to his honesty,

00:10:32.920 --> 00:10:34.840
and you were his only ally.

00:10:34.840 --> 00:10:35.480
But...

00:10:36.750 --> 00:10:41.610
You were the only one he allowed
to touch that Bösendorfer.

00:10:41.990 --> 00:10:42.970
Makoto?

00:10:43.730 --> 00:10:46.950
I was always left with the thankless tasks.

00:10:47.540 --> 00:10:50.870
How many times do you think
I had to apologise in his stead?!

00:10:52.100 --> 00:10:54.630
Overdue pay, lawsuits from his students...

00:10:54.630 --> 00:10:56.930
Even when Grandma left him!

00:10:56.930 --> 00:11:01.640
He made me say all the things
that were too hard for him!

00:11:01.640 --> 00:11:03.760
I understand what you went through.

00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:06.310
Excuse me? You're one to talk!

00:11:06.310 --> 00:11:10.580
Your disappearance is the worst one on my list!

00:11:10.580 --> 00:11:15.020
How could you walk away from conducting
the Berliner Symphoniker without any explanation?!

00:11:15.020 --> 00:11:18.780
Grandpa refused to apologise even then,

00:11:18.780 --> 00:11:21.680
so I had to kowtow to everyone involved.

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:24.840
I'd taken such good care of my fingers
so I could play the piano,

00:11:24.840 --> 00:11:26.910
but I had to stick them in
the ground over and over.

00:11:28.030 --> 00:11:30.910
I'm really sorry about that.

00:11:31.900 --> 00:11:35.040
There you go again, apologising
without actually saying anything.

00:11:35.790 --> 00:11:37.380
Whatever.

00:11:37.380 --> 00:11:39.630
I'm sick of all these tongue-tied people.

00:11:40.810 --> 00:11:44.810
He walked away from conducting the
Berliner Symphoniker and disappeared.

00:11:52.390 --> 00:11:55.640
Call for you, Kusakabe-sensei.

00:11:55.640 --> 00:11:57.360
I'm busy with a guest right now.

00:11:57.360 --> 00:11:57.980
Go.

00:11:58.830 --> 00:12:01.270
I want some alone time to think.

00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:03.230
All right.

00:12:21.700 --> 00:12:23.250
Tell me your names.

00:12:23.250 --> 00:12:24.470
Serizawa.

00:12:24.470 --> 00:12:25.320
Yamada.

00:12:25.320 --> 00:12:26.540
Tanaka.

00:12:26.540 --> 00:12:28.220
They're Kamijou-kun and Homura-san.

00:12:29.640 --> 00:12:31.680
Ah, from the concert band?

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:33.640
I'm not.

00:12:33.640 --> 00:12:37.950
I'd realised partway that someone
was eavesdropping on that conversation.

00:12:38.450 --> 00:12:40.690
Guess we can add trespassing
to the charges too.

00:12:40.690 --> 00:12:43.590
A-Are we going to be arrested?!

00:12:43.590 --> 00:12:47.440
I'm kidding. I'll keep this just between us.

00:12:47.440 --> 00:12:50.820
You talked about Sensei,
fully aware that we were listening?

00:12:51.760 --> 00:12:54.670
Shinjirou hasn't told you anything, right?

00:12:55.740 --> 00:13:00.730
Why play the melodica when you've had
such an elite music upbringing?

00:13:00.730 --> 00:13:02.340
You're Serizawa Naoko, yes?

00:13:03.530 --> 00:13:05.930
Come with me. I'll show you.

00:13:06.680 --> 00:13:12.110
Melodicas are delicate instruments that
adapt to your breathing and fingering,

00:13:12.110 --> 00:13:14.940
which allow you to express the ideas
in your head immediately in music form.

00:13:16.350 --> 00:13:19.190
Are the bumps on the back to prevent slippage?

00:13:19.190 --> 00:13:22.360
That's right, though those
aren't a standard feature.

00:13:22.360 --> 00:13:28.440
Back in sixth grade, I had surgery on my sweaty
fingers to help me better play the piano.

00:13:28.820 --> 00:13:31.220
Now my skin gets dry very easily.

00:13:31.220 --> 00:13:34.870
If you went that far,
why'd you give up on going pro?

00:13:35.920 --> 00:13:38.080
The talent and luck both eluded me.

00:13:38.580 --> 00:13:43.080
You say that when you placed so many times
in international piano competitions?

00:13:43.080 --> 00:13:47.080
In order to make a living as a pro,
you need to ride the winds of change

00:13:47.080 --> 00:13:49.630
and wait for Lady Luck to smile upon you.

00:13:50.260 --> 00:13:54.440
But you gave up without waiting for either.

00:13:54.440 --> 00:13:55.870
You're having doubts, aren't you?

00:13:56.350 --> 00:13:58.110
I heard about your hearing loss.

00:13:58.790 --> 00:14:00.100
We are weak beings.

00:14:00.860 --> 00:14:05.230
Whenever we're faced with adversity,
we blame it on what we've lost.

00:14:05.910 --> 00:14:09.280
And even if we eventually
recover our former skills,

00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:12.510
all our praise will be offset by the
caveat of "despite what you've lost".

00:14:13.160 --> 00:14:14.740
Would you be able to take feedback like that?

00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:19.910
Would you still love music and desire to go pro?

00:14:21.610 --> 00:14:24.790
There are two types of applause.

00:14:25.780 --> 00:14:28.800
The type that's steeped in pity and kindness,

00:14:29.210 --> 00:14:33.350
and the type sparked by emotions that
can only be expressed using one's body.

00:14:34.540 --> 00:14:38.260
You chose to go pro because
you've experienced the latter.

00:14:39.050 --> 00:14:39.640
Am I wrong?

00:14:41.890 --> 00:14:44.150
Why do you want to go pro, anyway?

00:14:45.630 --> 00:14:47.220
Why...?

00:14:47.220 --> 00:14:50.150
Do you want to reach a place where you
can focus on music, and music alone?

00:14:50.870 --> 00:14:52.860
Do you want to reach musical Eden?

00:14:54.620 --> 00:14:55.910
I...

00:14:58.810 --> 00:15:00.800
What're you doing here?

00:15:01.190 --> 00:15:05.700
I told her what you would've found too hard to.

00:15:06.510 --> 00:15:07.290
Makoto...

00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:12.320
Ever since you started teaching,
you've grown too soft on others.

00:15:12.320 --> 00:15:14.300
Give me back my clavietta.

00:15:16.730 --> 00:15:19.030
Mind helping me out a little tomorrow?

00:15:19.940 --> 00:15:22.140
There's something I want to check.

00:15:22.140 --> 00:15:23.600
Don't drag students into anything—

00:15:23.600 --> 00:15:27.220
It's all good. They owe me.

00:15:28.110 --> 00:15:30.950
I'll see you at noon tomorrow, then.

00:15:30.950 --> 00:15:33.390
Wait for me with some tasty bread.

00:15:37.230 --> 00:15:40.410
What music should I listen to in order to improve?

00:15:40.410 --> 00:15:44.430
If you want to go from zero to hero
in a couple of years, I recommend enka.

00:15:44.430 --> 00:15:46.100
What? Seriously?

00:15:46.100 --> 00:15:48.760
What kind of place is Moominvalley?

00:15:49.350 --> 00:15:52.810
Well, we've never had any
alumni come to teach us.

00:15:55.970 --> 00:15:57.210
There you are.

00:15:57.210 --> 00:15:58.980
Can we use the room next door?

00:15:58.980 --> 00:16:00.410
Of course!

00:16:00.410 --> 00:16:02.180
Go right on ahead.

00:16:02.180 --> 00:16:04.550
He's gonna have a lot of trouble
with women in the future.

00:16:04.550 --> 00:16:05.310
Yep.

00:16:08.200 --> 00:16:09.730
What are you doing?

00:16:10.110 --> 00:16:12.600
Oh, Nao-chan. What brings you here?

00:16:12.600 --> 00:16:13.940
I was asked to...

00:16:14.940 --> 00:16:16.830
Same here.

00:16:17.240 --> 00:16:20.330
Come on, Hiyama. Hurry up and grab some lunch.

00:16:20.330 --> 00:16:22.170
Sorry for disturbing you.

00:16:25.150 --> 00:16:27.770
The whereabouts of the Bösendorfer's key?

00:16:28.540 --> 00:16:31.650
Yeah. I don't recall him ever telling me.

00:16:31.650 --> 00:16:36.670
And the only thing Grandpa left me is this
clavietta he gave me just before he died.

00:16:36.670 --> 00:16:39.800
Is this an ivory keyboard too?

00:16:39.800 --> 00:16:43.890
No way. It's a substitute
that's made of natural casein.

00:16:43.890 --> 00:16:48.280
But it's just like an ivory keyboard
in that it discolours with sweat,

00:16:48.280 --> 00:16:50.150
so it's difficult to maintain.

00:16:51.400 --> 00:16:55.690
This clavietta was custom-made for me.

00:16:55.690 --> 00:16:58.040
It's an inheritance that
symbolises my Grandpa's pity.

00:16:58.040 --> 00:16:59.600
Pity?

00:16:59.600 --> 00:17:03.990
You see, there was just this one time
when I lashed out at him,

00:17:04.500 --> 00:17:08.270
asking him why he would only let
Shinjirou play the Bösendorfer.

00:17:09.050 --> 00:17:11.520
He probably remembered that

00:17:11.520 --> 00:17:15.790
and swapped this clavietta's keyboard
out for one made with an ivory substitute.

00:17:16.970 --> 00:17:20.870
Why didn't you ask Kusakabe-sensei for help

00:17:20.870 --> 00:17:22.370
instead of Serizawa-san?

00:17:23.010 --> 00:17:27.190
Shinjirou has grown far kinder
than I remember him.

00:17:27.940 --> 00:17:32.810
If Grandpa left any unkind words
for me in the clavietta,

00:17:32.810 --> 00:17:35.360
there's no guarantee Shinjirou will
read them out for me as they are.

00:17:36.270 --> 00:17:38.860
Because you can't read them
by yourself, you mean?

00:17:39.990 --> 00:17:43.440
When did your eyesight go so bad
that it became impossible to fix?

00:17:44.730 --> 00:17:47.540
You have one corner of your business card cut

00:17:47.540 --> 00:17:50.000
so you don't mistake the front and back.

00:17:50.420 --> 00:17:52.760
The reason you gave
Serizawa-san all that tough love

00:17:52.760 --> 00:17:55.700
was because you saw yourself in her, was it not?

00:17:56.840 --> 00:17:58.550
And when you came to
the principal's office yesterday,

00:17:59.250 --> 00:18:02.230
you couldn't tell who we were
until you heard our voices,

00:18:02.230 --> 00:18:04.480
and that's why you asked for
our names out of the blue, right?

00:18:05.270 --> 00:18:09.510
I was a college freshman when a disease
weakened my eyesight drastically.

00:18:10.150 --> 00:18:12.470
Now I can't even read anymore.

00:18:12.470 --> 00:18:14.240
That includes sheet music, of course.

00:18:14.810 --> 00:18:18.960
The doctors have told me
I might go totally blind someday.

00:18:18.960 --> 00:18:20.250
No way...

00:18:20.830 --> 00:18:22.610
When the disease came to light,

00:18:22.610 --> 00:18:24.870
my family and friends, with tears in their eyes,

00:18:24.870 --> 00:18:28.560
tried to cheer me up by saying I still
had a life in music even if I couldn't see.

00:18:29.220 --> 00:18:35.800
But Grandpa and Shinjirou alone
told me to give up on music.

00:18:36.500 --> 00:18:38.260
Sensei did?

00:18:38.260 --> 00:18:40.950
Their words were beyond cruel.

00:18:40.950 --> 00:18:45.090
But I could tell they spoke them
out of genuine concern for me.

00:18:46.790 --> 00:18:48.280
Serizawa-san,

00:18:48.280 --> 00:18:51.590
I don't pity you for your hearing loss.

00:18:51.590 --> 00:18:53.100
When faced with adversity,

00:18:53.810 --> 00:18:56.700
we're not the only ones who
blame it on what we've lost.

00:18:57.610 --> 00:18:59.980
Don't give in to their pity, okay?

00:19:00.550 --> 00:19:04.120
It's entirely up to you whether
you see it as a curse or a blessing.

00:19:04.970 --> 00:19:05.950
Right...

00:19:08.800 --> 00:19:09.550
Right!

00:19:10.170 --> 00:19:13.770
Oh, well. Guess we couldn't find the key after all.

00:19:13.770 --> 00:19:15.170
Hold on a second.

00:19:15.540 --> 00:19:19.380
Don't you want to know the last words
Yamanobe Fujihiko left you?

00:19:19.880 --> 00:19:21.150
Of course I do.

00:19:21.150 --> 00:19:24.070
If I could hear his voice one more time, I'd love to.

00:19:24.070 --> 00:19:27.000
You said earlier that maintaining
this clavietta was difficult, right?

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:30.980
Yeah. Bleaching the discoloured keys
takes time and effort.

00:19:30.980 --> 00:19:33.550
Do you only bleach the keyboard?

00:19:33.550 --> 00:19:35.850
What else would I bleach?

00:19:35.850 --> 00:19:41.550
I just noticed earlier that some of the bumps
on the back are of a different colour.

00:19:43.950 --> 00:19:45.340
It's true!

00:19:45.340 --> 00:19:47.780
Try touching them carefully with your fingers.

00:19:48.450 --> 00:19:50.800
Are there any parts that feel different?

00:19:51.540 --> 00:19:58.210
I think Yamanobe Fujihiko left his dear
granddaughter a message in Braille.

00:19:59.150 --> 00:20:01.930
"I... lo...

00:20:02.680 --> 00:20:04.810
st... the...

00:20:05.690 --> 00:20:08.000
ke... y..."

00:20:13.920 --> 00:20:15.840
"I lost the key"?!

00:20:15.840 --> 00:20:20.360
Making me say what he found
too hard to until the bitter end...

00:20:20.360 --> 00:20:22.920
These are definitely Grandpa's words.

00:20:24.940 --> 00:20:28.070
I guess I'll clean up after that geezer one last time.

00:20:28.070 --> 00:20:29.620
I'll go tell Shinjirou.

00:20:30.230 --> 00:20:31.540
Thank you!

00:20:31.540 --> 00:20:33.510
I owe you one!

00:20:44.900 --> 00:20:48.770
So when they dismantled the Bösendorfer's lock,

00:20:48.770 --> 00:20:52.010
they found the entire keyboard
replaced with brand-new ceramic.

00:20:52.010 --> 00:20:54.250
Wait, so what happened to the ivory keyboard?

00:20:54.250 --> 00:20:55.930
It was put on the clavietta.

00:20:55.930 --> 00:20:57.690
That was real ivory!

00:20:57.690 --> 00:20:58.940
What a waste!

00:20:58.940 --> 00:21:04.280
He obliged his sick granddaughter
in her one and only selfish wish.

00:21:04.280 --> 00:21:05.620
Such a touching story.

00:21:05.620 --> 00:21:06.900
Such a waste!

00:21:06.900 --> 00:21:10.780
Let's go see the fireworks, Chika-senpai!

00:21:10.780 --> 00:21:13.660
Naru-senpai and Maren-senpai are here too!

00:21:13.660 --> 00:21:16.610
Will you be okay with the noise of the fireworks?

00:21:16.610 --> 00:21:19.670
I've heard that loud noises aren't
good for people with hearing loss.

00:21:19.670 --> 00:21:24.670
But you still came to hear us perform
at both the district and prefectural qualifiers.

00:21:25.230 --> 00:21:29.230
And you could hear us well enough
in all this hustle and bustle.

00:21:30.320 --> 00:21:32.610
My right ear is a lost cause,

00:21:32.610 --> 00:21:35.490
but it looks like I can regain nearly
all hearing in my left ear by fall.

00:21:35.490 --> 00:21:38.270
I haven't taken my hearing aid out yet
because I'm still afraid, though.

00:21:39.820 --> 00:21:41.100
That's great news.

00:21:41.710 --> 00:21:46.400
The doctor was really shocked, too.
Said there might be a psychological cause behind it.

00:21:46.820 --> 00:21:51.280
I wonder if it's because
she freed me from my curse.

00:21:51.750 --> 00:21:53.280
Have you told Chika-chan?

00:21:53.280 --> 00:21:57.630
Not yet, because I haven't decided
on what I'm going to do.

00:21:57.630 --> 00:21:59.600
We won't take no for an answer, you know.

00:21:59.600 --> 00:22:00.790
I'm aware.

00:22:00.790 --> 00:22:04.000
Let's go, Haruta, Nao-chan!

00:22:04.930 --> 00:22:07.150
Don't call me that!

00:23:31.430 --> 00:23:32.880
I'll go and tell him how I feel.

00:23:32.880 --> 00:23:34.420
Give me some of your energy!

00:23:34.420 --> 00:23:36.390
He wouldn't abandon us and leave, right?

00:23:36.390 --> 00:23:38.730
Youth is a one-way ticket.

00:23:39.350 --> 00:23:40.890
Let's put on a good performance.
